<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.StyleGen</RootNamespace>
        <AssemblyName>Vixen.StyleGen</AssemblyName>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>

        <!--
            Shipped, but inside somebody else's package rather than as a package of its own. The
            build step is not a thing anybody installs on purpose — it is what
            `Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities.targets` runs, and the two have to be the same version or a
            stylesheet is generated by one release against tokens read by another.
            `Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities.csproj` packs this into its own `tools/`, which is the same
            arrangement `Vixen.Sdk` uses to version-lock the CLI it calls.
        -->
        <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>

        <!--
            An apphost would be a second file per RID to place in the package and nothing would run
            it: the .targets invokes `dotnet "…/Vixen.StyleGen.dll"`, which is the same shape
            `Vixen.Sdk.targets` uses for the CLI and which works on every platform the SDK does.
        -->
        <UseAppHost>false</UseAppHost>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            ⚠ **Why this is a process and not an `IIncrementalGenerator`. The YamlDotNet argument
            that used to be written here is dead — do not cite it.** `ThemeTokens` read YAML through
            `Vixen.Core.Yaml` until `@theme` replaced it; `Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities` has no
            `PackageReference` at all now, so "an analyzer's dependencies do not travel with it" no
            longer decides anything. The decision was re-taken on measurements in 2026-08, and these
            are the numbers rather than the argument. See README § "Why a process and not a source
            generator" for the workings.

            **What a generator would save: ~52 ms.** That is the `dotnet` host start plus assembly
            load, measured as the difference between a run with 43 scan files (~106 ms) and one with
            none (~52 ms), twelve runs each. It is paid once per build *that changed a scanned file*
            and **zero times otherwise** — the `.stamp` in the .targets makes MSBuild skip the whole
            target, which a no-change rebuild confirms.

            **What a generator would still pay: ~20 ms, per keystroke.** Scanning is ~11 ms across
            all 43 files and ~1 ms for one, so a per-file `IncrementalValuesProvider` caches it
            properly. `UtilityGenerator.Generate` is the other ~20 ms and it runs after `.Collect()`,
            over the whole candidate set — and that set is *designed* to be unstable: the scanner
            parses nothing, so `Vixen.Editor.Ui` yields 7 262 candidates of which 7 173 match no
            family at all. It is scanning English out of comments, so very nearly any edit changes
            the set and the collected stage re-runs. Trading 52 ms once per changed build for 20 ms
            per keystroke, in each of the six projects that use the step, is the wrong way round.

            ⚠ **And two blockers the YamlDotNet one was hiding are still live.** This assembly is
            net10.0 and an analyzer must be netstandard, so the only route is *linking* the 5 995
            lines of `Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities` under a second language surface — available, unlike
            for YamlDotNet, because it is Vixen's own code and uses nothing outside the BCL, but a
            real cost that `Vixen.Ui.Markup.Generators` pays and documents. And RS1035 bans
            `System.IO` inside an analyzer — all thirteen generators in this tree enforce it, and a
            generator writing files would be writing them on every keystroke from the IDE's generator
            host anyway — so keeping the `.vcss` and the report would mean keeping an MSBuild step,
            which erases the saving.

            So the build step runs where the implementation already runs — out of process, at
            net10.0, against the shipped assembly and not a second copy of it.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities\Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.StyleGen.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
